 ### Transformer Proposer
-Uses a transformer encoder to process input features and a transformer decoder with $N_f$ learned queries to produce proposals via cross-attention. Chosen when high accuracy in feature interaction is needed [S1, unofficial].
+A transformer encoder processes the input features, and a transformer decoder produces the final set of proposals via cross-attention with $N_f$ learned queries [S1, unofficial]. It is one of several architectures considered, all designed to be small relative to the coarse and fine networks to maintain negligible computational overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Pool Proposer
-Sums point features with positional encodings, passes through an FC layer and ReLU, averages to form a ray representation, then decodes via an FC. Chosen for lower computational overhead compared to Transformers [S1, unofficial].
+Each point feature is summed with the positional encoding of its location along the ray, passed through a fully connected layer and ReLU, averaged to form a single ray representation vector, and decoded via a fully connected layer to produce proposals [S1, unofficial]. It is one of several architectures considered, all designed to be small relative to the coarse and fine networks to maintain negligible computational overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### MLPMix Proposer
-Applies a single MLPMix block to stacked point features (sorted by position), followed by average pooling and an FC decoder. Chosen as a middle-ground between Pool and Transformer complexity [S1, unofficial].
+A single MLPMix block is applied to the stacked point features sorted by their position along the ray, followed by average pooling to get the ray representation, then proposals are decoded via a fully connected layer [S1, unofficial]. It is one of several architectures considered, all designed to be small relative to the coarse and fine networks to maintain negligible computational overhead [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the computational cost of the proposer network to the standard NeRF rendering time. The proposer is designed to be small relative to coarse/fine networks to have negligible overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the computational cost of the proposer network to the standard NeRF rendering time, but the proposer is designed to be small relative to coarse/fine networks to have negligible overhead [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing coarse features for all $N_c$ samples to pass to the proposer [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** Achieves superior view synthesis quality over NeRF and state-of-the-art on synthetic Blender benchmarks and comparable performance on real LLFF-NeRF scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiability allows learning optimal sampling strategies; can achieve 25% computation savings by filtering low-importance samples without quality loss [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Training from scratch is unstable; requires a two-stage training strategy [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiability allows learning optimal sampling strategies; predicted sample importance enables a 25% saving in computation without significantly sacrificing rendering quality [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Training from scratch is unstable due to weak supervision; requires a two-stage training strategy (pre-training to mimic vanilla NeRF, then fine-tuning) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF-ID was introduced in the paper "NeRF in detail: Learning to sample for view synthesis" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of vanilla NeRF's coarse-to-fine sampling, which relies on a heuristic piece-wise constant PDF computed from coarse densities [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to enable end-to-end training of the sampling mechanism, improving view synthesis quality [S1, unofficial].
+NeRF-ID was introduced in the paper "NeRF in detail: Learning to sample for view synthesis" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of vanilla NeRF's coarse-to-fine sampling, which relies on a heuristic piece-wise constant probability density function computed from coarse densities [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to enable end-to-end training of the sampling mechanism, improving scene representations and view synthesis quality [S1, unofficial].
 
